  "notes": "The lane's concept-album maximalist, and the entry gives it \"the widest band here, because the up-tempo funk material sits genuinely fast, while the ballads drop to 62–78\" — so the tempo runs 62–132 with the mode up at 104. Drums are the stated James-Brown-lineage funk kit: \"live funk kit played tight and fast — kick on 1 and the 'and' of 2, snare hard on 2&4, continuous 16th hats with offbeat pedal opening, handclaps and cowbell; James-Brown-lineage stop-time hits used as section markers\". That is `secondaryAnchor` 2&, a kick density of 3–6, a continuous 16th hat at fill density 0.72, `openHat.prob` 0.48 on all four offbeats, cowbell and snap lanes, and `fillBeforeSection` for the stop-time. Harmony is the entry's two-mode description — \"minor-mode funk vamps (dorian, 2-bar) for the up-tempos against lush major-key writing with maj7/maj9 and real modulation for the ballads\" — held in one spread rather than split into modes, so the triad, seventh and ninth weights are all substantial and the harmonic rhythm ranges from vamp to one-per-bar. Melody is \"wide-range (chest to full soprano), high note-density, with spoken/shouted interjections and choral group-vocal chants as the hook\", hence the three-octave register, density 5–11, and a call-and-response structure. Bass is \"slapped or picked 16ths, syncopated against the kick and mixed forward\". ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are all drum probabilities and velocities, the melody figures, and the harmonic weights; the research marks the tempo bands as inference and [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The orchestral-overture and suite habits are encoded only as long intros and outros — a single model cannot change key or metre mid-track.",
